+     import soundness.*

-     val r"$prefix([a-z0-9._%+-]+)@$domain([a-z0-9.-]+)\.$tld([a-z]{2,6})" =
+     val r"$prefix([a-z0-9._%+-]+)@$domain([a-z0-9.-]+)\.$tld([a-z]{2,6})" =

-       t"test@example.com": @unchecked
+       t"test@example.com": @unchecked

-     List(prefix, domain, tld)   // List(t"test", t"example", t"com")
+     List(prefix, domain, tld)   // List(t"test", t"example", t"com")

=     List(prefix, domain, tld)   // List(t"test", t"example", t"com")
-     ```
+     ```

-     Inside `r"…"` a backslash is a backslash — only the regular expression's own escaping
-     applies, so a literal parenthesis is `r"\("` — and `$` is written `$$` to mean itself. A
-     pattern which does not parse is a compile error naming the reason and pointing at the
-     offending character, _[↯/s.34.1.1] the regular expression could not be parsed because a
-     capturing group was not closed at 11_; a `$name` with no group after it is
-     _[↯/s.34.1.2] … a capturing group was expected immediately following an extractor_. Under
-     `regexBackends.re2` the pattern is also checked against the RE2 subset, so a
-     backreference or a lookaround is rejected at compile time rather than at its first use.
+     Dentro de `r"…"` una barra invertida es una barra invertida —solo se aplica el escape propio
+     de la expresión regular, así que un paréntesis literal es `r"\("`— y `$` se escribe `$$` para
+     significarse a sí mismo. Un patrón que no se puede analizar es un error de compilación que
+     nombra el motivo y señala el carácter culpable, _[↯/s.34.1.1] the regular expression could
+     not be parsed because a capturing group was not closed at 11_; un `$name` sin grupo detrás es
+     _[↯/s.34.1.2] … a capturing group was expected immediately following an extractor_. Con
+     `regexBackends.re2`, el patrón se comprueba además frente al subconjunto de RE2, así que una
+     referencia hacia atrás o un lookaround se rechazan en tiempo de compilación y no en su primer
+     uso.

=     backreference or a lookaround is rejected at compile time rather than at its first use.
=
-     ```scala
+     ```scala

=     backreference or a lookaround is rejected at compile time rather than at its first use.
